MF=6  Products Energy-angle Distributions                         9543 1451  145
   -----------------------------------------------                9543 1451  146
                                                                  9543 1451  147
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     9543 1451  148
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   9543 1451  149
photon production cross sections. We thus do not use MF12-15 for  9543 1451  150
the photon production that accompanies each reaction. All data    9543 1451  151
are generated with TALYS.                                         9543 1451  152
                                                                  9543 1451  153
   MT=5   (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions      9543 1451  154
                                                                  9543 1451  155
For energies below 20 MeV, MT5 contains the relative yields of    9543 1451  156
the (n,gamma x) reaction, where x may represent neutron, proton,  9543 1451  157
deuteron, triton, Helium-3 or alpha. Using the (n,gamma x) cross  9543 1451  158
section of MF3/MT3, the (n,gamma p), ..., (n,gamma alpha) cross   9543 1451  159
section can be recovered. For energies above 20 MeV, MT5 contains 9543 1451  160
the production yields of particles and residual products. It also 9543 1451  161
contains the secondary energy-angle distributions for all         9543 1451  162
particles and photons. First, the yields for neutrons are given   9543 1451  163
for the whole energy range. Next, on a secondary energy grid the  9543 1451  164
relative emission spectra are given together with the parameters  9543 1451  165
for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. Inelastic  9543 1451  166
scattering cross sections for discrete states have been broadened 9543 1451  167
and added to the continuum spectra. This procedure is repeated    9543 1451  168
for protons, deuterons, tritons, Helium-3, alpha particles and    9543 1451  169
photons. Finally, the residual production yields are given per    9543 1451  170
final product. All these yields and relative distributions can    9543 1451  171
be multiplied with the cross sections given in MF3/MT5 to get     9543 1451  172
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     9543 1451  173
sections.                                                         9543 1451  174
                                                                  9543 1451  175
   MT=16   (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production       9543 1451  176
                                                                  9543 1451  177
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  9543 1451  178
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 9543 1451  179
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          9543 1451  180
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       9543 1451  181
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 9543 1451  182
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     9543 1451  183
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  9543 1451  184
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      9543 1451  185
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         9543 1451  186
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             9543 1451  187
                                                                  9543 1451  188
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   9543 1451  189
followed:                                                         9543 1451  190
                                                                  9543 1451  191
   MT=17   (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production       9543 1451  192
   MT=22   (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production       9543 1451  193
